; exit 53 (see .exit)
; MM-LIFE-2c event 3, the subset that needs no escape analysis to be
; sound: a `let` binding whose initialiser is a DIRECT CONSTRUCTION and
; whose name is only ever the object of a field READ is released when
; its scope ends.
;
; Both conditions are syntactic on purpose. The rule as specified is
; stated over an escape analysis this compiler does not have; what it
; does have is a shape whose answer needs none - the binding holds the
; block's birth share and no one else's, and a field read answers a
; field, which took its own share at construction (event 6) if it is
; itself a reference. The walker that decides the second condition
; DEFAULTS TO ESCAPES: a tag it does not recognise answers "escapes",
; so widening the surface is a deliberate edit rather than an
; omission, and the failure direction is the leak rather than the
; early free.
;
;   32  20,000 build-read-drop calls move the allocator's bump by 256
;       bytes. The same loop before this event moved it by 640,224 -
;       one 32-byte block per call, never reclaimed
;   16  THE CONTROL, which must go the other way: the same loop with
;       the record RETURNED instead of read grows by 160,224 bytes
;       over 5,000 calls, because the name is the let's own value and
;       the walker refuses to release it. A rule that released this
;       one would be a use-after-free, and a control that cannot fail
;       is not a control
;    4  the value is still right - the release is emitted after the
;       body, not before it
;    1  every step above ran
;
; What this event does NOT reach, measured rather than assumed: the
; compiler's own IR gains ZERO release sites under it (112 before, 112
; after, over 36,000 lines). 13 of 2,337 `let` bindings in
; `self_host/` and `stdlib/` bind a direct construction at all, and
; none survives the escape test - the corpus builds its records through
; `mk*` functions returning `Int`-declared handles, which is the same
; reason MM-LIFE-2e's acceptance measurements cannot move. The event is
; for programs, not for this one.
;
; The unfixed compiler answers 21: the two flatness claims fail and the
; control passes, which is what a compiler that releases nothing looks
; like.
(import Mem)

(struct Pair
  (a : Int)
  (b : Int))

(:: b2i (-> Bool Int))

(fn (b2i x)
  (if x
    1
    0
  )
)

; the shape event 3 owns: build, read fields, drop
(:: score (-> Int Int))

(fn (score n)
  (let ((p (Pair n n)))
    (+ p.a p.b)
  )
)

; the same build, but the record IS the value - it escapes, and the
; walker must refuse to release it
(:: keep (-> Int Pair))

(fn (keep n)
  (let ((p (Pair n n)))
    p
  )
)

(:: readA (-> Pair Int))

(fn (readA p) p.a)

(:: sumDropped (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (sumDropped n acc)
  (if (<= n 0)
    acc
    (sumDropped (- n 1) (+ acc (score n)))
  )
)

(:: sumEscaped (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (sumEscaped n acc)
  (if (<= n 0)
    acc
    (sumEscaped (- n 1) (+ acc (readA (keep n))))
  )
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (mut acc 0)
    (mut steps 0)
  )
    {
      (let ((b1 (memAlloc 200)))
        {
          (sumDropped 20000 0)
          (let ((b2 (memAlloc 200)))
            (set acc (+ acc (* 32 (b2i (< (- b2 b1) 4096)))))
          )
          (set steps (+ steps 1))
        }
      )
      (let ((c1 (memAlloc 200)))
        {
          (sumEscaped 5000 0)
          (let ((c2 (memAlloc 200)))
            (set acc (+ acc (* 16 (b2i (> (- c2 c1) 100000)))))
          )
          (set steps (+ steps 1))
        }
      )
      (set acc (+ acc (* 4 (b2i (== (score 7) 14)))))
      (set steps (+ steps 1))
      (if (== steps 3)
        (+ acc 1)
        acc
      )
    }
  )
)
